<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Count Egmont</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lamoral, Count of Egmont (1522–1568), was a general and statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, celebrated for his victories over the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> in 1557 and Gravelines in 1558. With William of Orange and Count Horne he led the noble opposition to Philip II's policies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> had Egmont and Horne beheaded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1568, which fuelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> and inspired Goethe's play Egmont.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont.jpg" alt="Lamoral, Count of Egmont" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Pourbus the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Lamoral,_Count_of_Egmont,_Prince_of_Gavere_(1522%E2%80%931568),_by_Frans_Pourbus_the_Elder.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lamoral, Count of Egmont and Prince of Gavere, was born on 18 November 1522 at the Château de Lahamaide near Ellezelles. He headed one of the wealthiest and most powerful families of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, a branch of which had ruled the sovereign duchy of Guelders until 1538. His father was John IV of Egmont, a knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-the-golden-fleece">Order of the Golden Fleece</a>, and his mother belonged to a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a>, from which he inherited the title of prince de Gavere. He received a military education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and in 1542 inherited his elder brother Charles's estates in Holland.</p><p>In 1544, in the presence of the Holy Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and the Archduke Ferdinand I, he married Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> of Palatinate-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a>, whose brother became the Elector Palatine Frederick III. The match raised the family's standing further.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Egmont became Captain General of the Lowlands under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, a knight of the Golden Fleece in 1546 and an Imperial Chamberlain. In the service of the Spanish army he defeated the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> in 1557 and at Gravelines in 1558. In 1559 he was made stadtholder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Artois at the age of 37.</p><p>As a member of the Council of State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, he joined William, Prince of Orange, and the Count of Horn in protesting against the introduction of the Inquisition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> by Cardinal Antoine Perrenot Granvelle. In 1565, short of funds because he had maintained the representation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> from his own pocket, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> to ask Philip II for a change of policy and received little more than courtesy. A devout Catholic, he deplored the Beeldenstorm of iconoclasm in the churches and stayed loyal to the king, though he and Horn still opposed the Inquisition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, William of Orange fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. Egmont ignored his warning, and Alba had him and Horn arrested on charges of heresy and held in a castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>. Appeals for clemency came from sovereigns across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-the-golden-fleece">Order of the Golden Fleece</a> and from the Emperor Maximilian II, without effect. Both men were beheaded before the Town Hall on the Grand-Place in Brussels on 5 June 1568, and Egmont's dignity on the scaffold was widely noted. Their deaths set off protests across the Netherlands and fed the resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>He is buried in the crypt of the Church of the Assumption of Our Lady at Zottegem. A statue of the two counts stands on the Petit Sablon square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. Goethe made him the hero of the play Egmont, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a> composed an overture and incidental music in 1810.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lamoral, Count of Egmont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spanish forces led by <b>Lamoral, Count of Egmont</b>, defeated the French army of Marshal Paul de Thermes at Gravelines, near Calais.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duke of Alba had Horne and <b>Egmont</b> beheaded in Brussels in 1568, a spark of the Dutch Revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-horne">County of Horne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lahamaide, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Grand-Place, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.7,3.73&amp;year=1551" aria-label="Lahamaide on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4635 404 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lamoral, Count of Egmont"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5085 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5085 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.7,3.73&amp;year=1551">What was here in 1551? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
